thesis:{
image:"assets/images/projects/thesis-profiles.png",kicker:"INDIVIDUAL MASTER'S DISSERTATION · KU LEUVEN / VUB",
title:"Resolving Suspended Sediment Concentration Profiles in Uniform Open-channel Flow with a 1DV Model",
intro:"An individual study of how a vertical one-dimensional model represents suspended sediment concentration in steady, uniform open-channel flow.",
context:"The classical Rouse profile requires a finite reference concentration near the bed. This dissertation evaluates how a numerical 1DV model reproduces the concentration profile above its lower boundary using measurements from Cellino (1998).",
approach:"I implemented and calibrated a MATLAB 1DV k–ε model with four sediment-diffusion formulations. Calibration used the shape of measured velocity profiles. Concentration and independent Reynolds-stress and turbulence diagnostics were evaluated after calibration.",
contribution:"This was my individual master's dissertation. I implemented and calibrated the model, analysed the profiles from 17 experiments, compared the closures with the Rouse reference, and assembled the results in an Excel workbook.",
outcome:"The model captured the overall concentration decrease above its lower boundary. Median concentration NRMSE across the four closures ranged from 32.9% to 38.6%, a 5.7 percentage-point spread. Reynolds-stress and turbulence checks also exposed limitations that velocity fit alone could not show.",
tags:"1DV MATLAB model · k–ε turbulence · Rouse profile · 17 Cellino experiments · Four sediment closures · Excel",
images:[{src:"assets/images/projects/thesis-profiles.png",alt:"Measured and modelled velocity and suspended-sediment concentration profiles for Run 6",captionKey:"thesisSlide1"},{src:"assets/images/projects/thesis-accuracy.png",alt:"Concentration error across 17 experiments and four sediment closures",captionKey:"thesisSlide2"}]
},
